WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.980
Recent advancements in technology

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.210
have allowed animators to
push the limits of their work,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.610
resulting in more creative and detailed

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.620
films and TV shows than ever before.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.760
To find out how six of these stunning

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.140
and unique animations were created,

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.640
let's take a look behind the scenes.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.300
Stop-motion-animated movies

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.750
have been around for over 100 years,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.960
but the production house Laika Studios

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.060
brought this animation
style into the 21st century

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.260
by utilizing 3D printing,

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.350
a technique that is especially prevalent

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.527
in their 2016 masterpiece,

00:00:47.527 --> 00:00:49.920
"Kubo and the Two Strings."

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.560
Traditionally,
stop-motion-animated characters

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.660
had to be made of a moldable
material, such as clay,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.370
so that the faces of the
characters could be changed.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.530
However, by using 3D-printed faces,

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.540
Laika Studios is able to
alter the facial expressions

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.110
of its characters more
quickly and consistently.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:12.350
Each expression is created
using 3D-modeling software.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:15.495
The models are then printed
with the 3D printer,

00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:18.000
and magnets are added
to the back of the face

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.859
so they can later be
attached to the puppets.

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:24.380
There were approximately 48
million different combinations

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.110
of facial expressions created for

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:28.350
the film's main character, Kubo.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.100
His face consists of a top

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.450
and bottom faceplate that combine.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.700
The tiny seam between the two pieces

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.290
was painted out in postproduction.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.760
Because there were so many faces,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.220
each face had a code on the back of it

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.910
in order to keep all of them organized.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.487
Advanced 3D-printing
technology also allowed

00:01:47.487 --> 00:01:48.760
"Kubo and the Two Strings"

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.890
to be able to create intricate puppets

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.460
that would be impossible
to create otherwise,

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.950
such as the character called Moon Beast,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.130
who was Laika's first
completely 3D-printed character.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.880
Moon Beast was made of 881

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.450
individual 3D-printed parts:

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.517
130 color parts and 751 components

00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:12.223
for the armatures used
in its body and legs.

00:02:14.477 --> 00:02:18.120
"Loving Vincent" is the first
feature-length animated film

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.800
in which every frame was hand
painted using oil on canvas -

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.060
approximately 65,000 frames in total.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.330
The film was painted in the
style of Vincent van Gogh,

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.410
featuring 94 of the
artist's original paintings

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.030
as the settings for the story.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.730
In order to create the film,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.650
the production team invented
a special animation process.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.780
First they filmed actors on a blue stage,

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.970
with background elements
digitally added later.

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.440
Then one of the 125 highly
skilled artists on the team

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.390
painted over each frame of
this live-action footage

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.350
in the style of Van Gogh.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.430
The paintings were then
photographed and assembled

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.200
to complete the animation process.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.430
The producers faced several challenges

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.630
in this ambitious project.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.090
First, they had to change
the color palette of

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.260
several of Van Gogh's paintings,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.770
because while Van Gogh painted
across all four seasons,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.910
the film's plot takes
place only in the summer.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:19.370
Additionally, Van Gogh experimented with

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:22.020
several different styles
throughout his life,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.160
so characters had to
be painted differently

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.750
based on which Van Gogh painting

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:28.340
was being used as the backdrop.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.160
When the production team
realized they needed

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.390
to hire more painters to
finish the project on time,

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.590
they discovered that there
literally weren't enough

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.900
trained painters available in Poland,

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.810
where the film was being made.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.520
So they posted a job listing

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.430
and ended up hiring 45 oil
painters from around the world,

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.780
sifting through 5,000 applications.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.370
Given all of these factors,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.370
it's no surprise that it took the team

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:55.510
about five years to complete the project.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.437
The film's director said that they had

00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:00.167
"without a doubt invented the slowest form

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:02.477
of filmmaking ever devised."

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.960
It took producers over a year to create

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.780
just 10 seconds of test animation footage

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.420
that they were happy with for

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.180
"Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse."

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:18.450
The eye-grabbing animation
style of the 2018 film

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.860
looks wildly different from
typical animated movies,

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.870
and it borrows many of its innovative

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.735
animation techniques from comic books.

00:04:26.735 --> 00:04:30.820
One of the most noticeable
differences involves frame rate.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.240
Animated films are typically
24 frames per second,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.367
and creating a different image for each

00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:40.127
frame is known as animating "on ones."

00:04:40.127 --> 00:04:42.210
"Spider-Verse" broke the mold

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.920
and animated much of the movie "on twos,"

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.590
meaning that they kept some
images on screen for two frames.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.131
Each character's pose lasts longer

00:04:51.131 --> 00:04:52.983
and is much more pronounced.

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:56.980
They alternated between
on twos and on ones,

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.360
depending on the nature of the scene.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.700
Another defining
characteristic of the film

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.430
is that there isn't any motion blur,

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.865
a CGI trick that most new films use

00:05:06.865 --> 00:05:10.070
to soften a movement and
make it seem more real.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:14.556
Instead, they used an old-school
technique called a smear.

00:05:14.556 --> 00:05:16.660
This was used a lot in early cartoons

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:18.550
to create the sense of motion.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.753
If you look at a single frame,

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:22.740
you'll notice things like multiple limbs

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.770
to create the illusion of movement.

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:27.190
The producers also decided not to create

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.550
a traditional depth of
field by using lens blurs

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.000
and instead utilized misprinting,

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.380
inspired by the common
error in comic books

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.100
when ink is misaligned.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.560
When something is in focus,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.630
the colors align and are crisper,

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:43.533
while the colors of background
objects are often offset.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.057
The characters in Netflix's
Oscar-nominated film

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:51.840
"Klaus" look three dimensional.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.000
But unlike most animated
movies these days,

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.180
the characters in "Klaus" aren't all CGI

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.120
and can't even be considered 3D.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.640
The characters were
first hand drawn, inked,

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:06.400
and painted using digital tablets.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.190
Then the artists were
able to paint with light,

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:13.350
thanks to software created
specifically for "Klaus."

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:15.000
The second major feature that helped

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.550
bring the characters to life

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:20.340
and added to the 3D
illusion was texturing.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:21.850
With another piece of software

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.560
they used contour, lighting, and motion

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.370
to add surface qualities to
specific parts of a character.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:31.500
For example, the skin could appear smooth

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.400
while the clothing would be rougher.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.630
In the end, the characters
appeared much more 3D

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.290
and immersed in their environment,

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.220
as opposed to looking like stickers

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:43.350
on top of an elaborately
painted background.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:47.140
While the majority of the
film followed this 2D process,

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.480
the animators did use 3D models

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:51.440
for some characters and objects

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.030
and combined the two styles seamlessly.

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:56.820
Even though these were created using CGI,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.460
they were lit the same way
as the 2D characters were.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:03.300
Scenes like this chase
scene at the end of the film

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.730
so seamlessly combined 2D and 3D elements

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:09.750
that it can be difficult
to tell which is which.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.550
While audiences and critics were divided

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.760
on Disney's 2019 "Lion King" remake,

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.880
it's undeniable that the
animation was a technical triumph,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.780
as it's the most photorealistic

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.370
animated major motion picture to date.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:28.320
One of the first steps
in most animation films

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:30.230
is pre-visualization,

00:07:30.230 --> 00:07:32.710
where an extremely low-resolution version

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.690
of the animation is created in order

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:38.020
to perfect character
movements and camera angles.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:41.660
However, "The Lion King" took
a new approach to this phase,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.140
creating simplified animated sequences

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.370
that could be viewed in virtual reality.

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:48.920
The director and production team

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:51.230
would then put on VR headsets,

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.260
which allowed them to actually step foot

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.100
in the virtual set that they had created.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.522
This allowed them more
freedom to explore each scene

00:07:59.522 --> 00:08:02.700
and let them easily set
up shots, adjust lighting,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.170
and choreograph movements.

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.560
This digital environment
helped them emulate

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.833
the nature-documentary aesthetic
that they were aiming for.

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:14.300
The "Lion King" animation team also ran

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.220
into the unique challenge
of having to make

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.230
the animals not only realistic,

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:20.780
but also able to sing and talk.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.430
In order to pull this off,

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.530
they timed the characters'
breathing to their dialogue

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:28.700
and would make the belly
muscles and diaphragm tighten.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.840
That way, it felt like the animals

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.860
were forcing air out of
their mouths as they spoke.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.880
Each layer of these animals also

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.260
went through a simulation pass,

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:42.640
an additional phase of
animation where skin details

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.950
such as muscle ripples, skin wrinkles,

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.290
and fur interaction are all added.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.040
Striking that balance between

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.330
staying true to the original film

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.490
and creating a hyperrealistically
animated "Lion King"

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:57.990
for a new audience was a
particularly tough challenge,

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:02.715
and it required 77 million
hours of animation rendering.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.770
The 2019 Amazon Prime series "Undone"

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.450
was created using rotoscoping,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.500
the process of drawing animations

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.320
over live-action video footage.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.464
Rotoscoping has been around
since the early 1900s,

00:09:18.464 --> 00:09:20.790
but the rotoscoping process for "Undone"

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.520
is so much more advanced

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:26.560
that it's practically unrecognizable
from its predecessors.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.790
The producers for "Undone" were originally

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:32.090
going to film the series
using live action,

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:35.250
but they ultimately decided
that using rotoscope animation

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.370
would give the series the surreal feeling

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.280
that it necessitated,

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.460
as the story revolves around a young woman

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:44.450
who has an unsteady grip on reality.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.000
The scenes were initially shot

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.180
with actors and minimal sets,

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:51.653
using grids on set as reference
points for the animators.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:54.770
There was no need for green screen

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:56.720
because every area of the frames

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.693
would be painted over during
the rotoscoping process.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.600
The live action was then rotoscoped,

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:06.130
with animators drawing crisp
lines over the footage.

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.400
The rotoscope animation
was then sent to Amsterdam,

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:11.720
where artists would use oil-based paints

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:14.375
to add colors and shadows to the footage.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:16.830
While many of the backgrounds were

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:19.890
painted in Amsterdam using oil on canvas,

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.970
some scenes required backgrounds that were

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.730
created using 3D-modeling software.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.630
To maintain the uniform painted look,

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:31.080
the 3D-animation team would
send a layout to the painters,

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.350
who would paint over the image.

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:35.290
After the painting was complete,

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.150
the 3D artists could then
use the finished painting

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:41.386
as a reference for texturing
the 3D environment.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:49.110
What film do you think we should
have included on the list?

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:52.130
What animated film would
you like us to discuss next?

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.763
Let us know in the comments.

